---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import BacktrackingLab from '../islands/BacktrackingLab.svelte';
---

<Base title="Why no backtracking">
    <PageHeader num="01" title="Why no backtracking" lede="Most regex engines are recursive-descent interpreters, and that design decision has a cost profile this library chose not to accept. Understanding it explains everything else." />

    <div class="prose prose-zinc max-w-none prose-headings:font-bold prose-a:text-teal-700 prose-code:before:content-none prose-code:after:content-none prose-code:rounded prose-code:bg-zinc-100 prose-code:px-1 prose-code:py-0.5 prose-code:text-[0.85em] prose-code:font-medium">
        <h2>How a backtracking engine works</h2>
        <p>
            The regex engine you probably use daily — <code>java.util.regex</code>, PCRE, Python's <code>re</code>, JavaScript's — is at heart a <em>recursive-descent interpreter</em> for your pattern. When it meets <code>a*</code> it makes a choice: consume one more <code>a</code>, or stop? When it meets <code>a|b</code> it chooses a branch. If a choice later leads to a dead end, it <em>backtracks</em>: undoes consumed characters, returns to the most recent choice point, and tries the other road.
        </p>
        <p>
            You can think of it as a depth-first search over "ways to match." DFS with an undo stack is a simple design — and it's why <code>group(1)</code> can just be "whatever the interpreter happened to write down when it walked through the group." It's also why these engines can support backreferences and lookaround, which automaton-based engines cannot: those features need memory of the match, and an interpreter has it naturally. But DFS has a property that matters at scale: its running time is proportional to the <em>number of paths</em>, not the length of the input.
        </p>
        <p>
            Now nest the choice points. <code>(a+)+b</code> against <code>"aaaa…a"</code> (no <code>b</code>): the outer <code>+</code> and inner <code>+</code> can split the run of a's between them in exponentially many ways. The engine dutifully tries every split, fails at the end of each, backtracks, and tries the next. Twenty-four a's is already tens of millions of steps. This is <a href="https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Regular_expression_Denial_of_Service_-_ReDoS" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ReDoS</a>, and it has taken down real services.
        </p>
    </div>

    <BacktrackingLab client:load />

    <div class="prose prose-zinc max-w-none prose-headings:font-bold prose-a:text-teal-700 prose-code:before:content-none prose-code:after:content-none prose-code:rounded prose-code:bg-zinc-100 prose-code:px-1 prose-code:py-0.5 prose-code:text-[0.85em] prose-code:font-medium">
        <h2>How other engines mitigate this</h2>
        <p>
            To be fair: current JDKs have tamed many of the classic blowups in <code>java.util.regex</code> (the lab above runs fine on a modern JDK, in linear-ish time, thanks to memoization-style fixes). PCRE has a match limit. .NET has timeouts. These work well in practice — but they are patches over the algorithm's shape. The alternative is an algorithm whose cost depends only on the input length, which is what this library opted for, at the price of dropping backreferences and lookaround entirely.
        </p>

        <h2>The automaton family, in three moves</h2>
        <p>
            There is a parallel lineage of regex engines — Ken Thompson's 1968 construction, the one in <code>grep</code>, RE2, Go's <code>regexp</code>, and re2j. The idea: compile the pattern to a small graph called an <strong>NFA</strong> (nondeterministic finite automaton — "a machine that can be in several states at once"), then run it by <em>tracking the set of all currently-possible states</em> as you read each character. Set size is bounded by pattern size, so matching is O(input × pattern). No path is ever explored twice, because you're not exploring paths at all — you're advancing a frontier.
        </p>
        <p>Trade-offs of each design, in one table:</p>
        <div class="not-prose my-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-lg border border-zinc-200">
            <table class="w-full text-sm">
                <thead>
                    <tr class="border-b border-zinc-200 bg-zinc-50 text-left text-xs tracking-wide text-zinc-500 uppercase">
                        <th class="px-4 py-2">Engine kind</th>
                        <th class="px-4 py-2">Time per match attempt</th>
                        <th class="px-4 py-2">Captures</th>
                        <th class="px-4 py-2">Can support backrefs / lookaround</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody class="text-zinc-700">
                    <tr class="border-b border-zinc-100">
                        <td class="px-4 py-2 font-medium">Backtracking (PCRE, j.u.r)</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-2">exponential worst case</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-2">trivially, as a side effect</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-2">yes (that's the point)</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr class="border-b border-zinc-100">
                        <td class="px-4 py-2 font-medium">NFA simulation (Pike VM)</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-2">O(input × pattern)</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-2">with per-thread tag copies</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-2">no</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td class="px-4 py-2 font-medium">DFA (this engine)</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-2">O(input) — one table lookup per char</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-2 text-teal-700 font-medium">the hard part — solved by TDFA</td>
                        <td class="px-4 py-2">no</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p>
            The catch with going full DFA has always been the middle column's second row: capture groups. A plain DFA state tells you "the pattern is still alive," but <em>forgets which path it took</em> — and captures are exactly a question about the path. Classic DFA engines either gave up on submatches (early grep) or fell back to a slower engine for them (some RE2 configurations, for leftmost-longest submatches).
        </p>

        <h2>TDFA: a DFA that remembers, by deciding early</h2>
        <p>
            A <strong>TDFA</strong> — <em>tagged</em> DFA — is a DFA where every transition can carry tiny side effects on a set of integer <em>registers</em>: "set register 2 to the current position," "copy register 5 into register 3," "set register 7 to nil." The compile-time analysis figures out, for every state and every character class, exactly which of those writes the <em>winning</em> path would have performed — and bakes them into the transition. At match time the DFA carries one flat <code>int[]</code>; when it accepts, the group boundaries are sitting in the final registers. No paths, no stacks, no replay.
        </p>
        <p>
            That analysis is the heart of this engine and of the paper it implements — Borsotti &amp; Trofimovich, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.01398" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>A closer look at TDFA</em></a> (2022). The next chapters walk through the whole pipeline: how the pattern becomes a tagged NFA, how subset construction turns that into a register-writing DFA, how a compiler-style register allocator shrinks the result, and how the finished automaton executes as JVM bytecode.
        </p>

        <h2>What this design buys, and what it costs</h2>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6">
            <li><strong>Linear match time by construction</strong> — there is no engine in the library that could burn match-time budget, so pathological patterns cannot either. The guarantee is structural, not a matter of patched known cases.</li>
            <li><strong>Predictable memory</strong> — a matcher owns one scratch <code>int[]</code> and optional memo tables, capped by a 16 MiB-per-pattern runtime budget.</li>
            <li><strong>Speed</strong> — a table walk is cheap per character. On short-input search the engine measures 2–6× faster than re2j, though <code>java.util.regex</code> still wins some literal-heavy scenarios (tracked in <code>BENCHMARKS.md</code>).</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            The price is paid once, at <code>Pattern.compile</code>: ~290 µs (interpreter) to ~1.3 ms (bytecode) per pattern, versus ~16 µs for <code>java.util.regex</code> — and backreferences, lookaround, atomic groups and possessive quantifiers are simply not supported, with no fallback engine to catch them. Compile eagerly, match often; in a pathological case, compilation fails cleanly with a "pattern too large" error instead of exhausting time and memory. Those limits are three system properties you can raise; see the <a href="/budgets/">Budgets</a> chapter.
        </p>
    </div>
</Base>
